<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
courier.xml -- the delivery mission, as a behaviour tree.

This is the bonus implementation. It replaces the leg loop inside
courier_server.cpp -- send a goal, time it out, retry it, move to the next leg
-- and nothing else. Booking, the action interface, the Nav2 client and the
feedback are the same in both versions; courier_bt_server.cpp runs this file
instead of the hand-written state machine.

Everything the tree needs is on the blackboard before the first tick, written
by the node from the frozen job and from courier.yaml:

    pickup_pose, dropoff_pose     Pose2D, resolved when the job was booked
    pickup_name, dropoff_name     string, for the feedback message
    max_attempts                  int,   max_retries + 1
    leg_timeout_msec              int,   leg_timeout * 1000
    retry_delay_msec              int,   retry_delay * 1000

The poses are on the blackboard rather than the names because a job's targets
are frozen at booking time. Looking a name up again here would let an edit to
courier.yaml move the destination of a delivery that had already been accepted.

Only GoToLocation is ours. Sequence, RetryUntilSuccessful, Fallback, Timeout,
Delay and AlwaysFailure are BehaviorTree.CPP built-ins, and between them they
are the whole reason to do this: they are the retry counter, the timeout timer
and the leg ordering that the state machine spells out by hand.

The two legs are written out rather than factored into a SubTree. It is eight
more lines and it means the entire mission is readable in one screen, which is
the property that makes a tree worth having.
-->
<root BTCPP_format="4" main_tree_to_execute="CourierDelivery">

  <BehaviorTree ID="CourierDelivery">
    <Sequence name="delivery">

      <!-- Leg 1: drive to the pickup.
           Sequence is what makes this a delivery rather than two errands: if
           the pickup fails the dropoff is never attempted, and the job ends
           reporting failed_leg "pickup". -->
      <RetryUntilSuccessful num_attempts="{max_attempts}" name="pickup_attempts">
        <Fallback>
          <Timeout msec="{leg_timeout_msec}">
            <GoToLocation pose="{pickup_pose}" name="{pickup_name}" leg="pickup"/>
          </Timeout>

          <!-- Reached only when the attempt above failed or timed out. Waits
               retry_delay, then fails, which sends RetryUntilSuccessful round
               again. The delay belongs here and not in front of the attempt:
               a Delay wrapping GoToLocation would also postpone the first try,
               and every delivery would start three seconds late. -->
          <Delay delay_msec="{retry_delay_msec}">
            <AlwaysFailure/>
          </Delay>
        </Fallback>
      </RetryUntilSuccessful>

      <!-- Leg 2: drive to the dropoff. Identical but for the target. -->
      <RetryUntilSuccessful num_attempts="{max_attempts}" name="dropoff_attempts">
        <Fallback>
          <Timeout msec="{leg_timeout_msec}">
            <GoToLocation pose="{dropoff_pose}" name="{dropoff_name}" leg="dropoff"/>
          </Timeout>
          <Delay delay_msec="{retry_delay_msec}">
            <AlwaysFailure/>
          </Delay>
        </Fallback>
      </RetryUntilSuccessful>

    </Sequence>
  </BehaviorTree>

</root>
